 "lineas": [
  {
   "texto": "You live on a farm where it barely rains for eight months a year.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you put a barrel under the roof to catch the rain.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "One storm fills it in minutes. A week later, it's empty again.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And all that open water turns into a mosquito nursery.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you dig a big hole and build a concrete tank underground.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "A gutter and a pipe carry every drop from the roof straight into it.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "But the first rain washes dust, leaves and bird droppings off the roof. Your water turns brown.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "So you add a first flush pipe. The dirtiest water fills it and never reaches the tank.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "And a screen on the gutter stops the leaves.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Then you seal it with a concrete lid. No sunlight, no mosquitoes.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Down there in the dark, the water stays cool and algae can't grow.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "When the tank is full, an overflow pipe sends the extra water away.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Forget the bucket. A hand pump brings the water up.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "In Brazil's dry northeast, families have built more than a million of these.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Each one holds sixteen thousand liters. Enough to drink and cook through the whole dry season.",
   "pausa": 0.3
  },
  {
   "texto": "Congratulations! You just turned a roof into a water supply. What should we build next?",
   "pausa": 0.3
  }
 ],
